//! The connection objects: `PipeInlet` and `PipeOutlet`, the two ends of a
//! connection, and `PipeReply` and `PipeRequest`, the one-shot pair one
//! exchange is made of (spec §2.1).
//!
//! One file holds both pairs of types, because every sentence that explains
//! either half of a pair explains it by contrast with the other. Neither type
//! of a pair names the other (an inlet has no way to reach its outlet; that is
//! the capability model working), so the module graph did not force this;
//! readability did. `PipeReply` is the client's claim on one reply and
//! `PipeRequest` is the server's obligation to send it. They are §2.1's
//! `PipeReplyHandle` / `PipeRequestHandle`, rendered short at the typed tier as
//! `MemoryObject` renders as `Memory`.
//!
//! It imports the floor, `sos.waiter` (the pipe `Waiter` overloads and
//! `Waiter.wait` live here) and the two region modules (a message can carry a
//! `Memory` or an `IoMemory`). The factory is `Process.pipe_create`, because it
//! needs a `Process`, which is declared in `sos.system`, which imports this
//! file. See there for the shape that ordering forces; it is the same one
//! `Process.map` has.
//!
//! `WouldBlock` is an error here, not an `Optional`. A caller that waits on the
//! level with a `Waiter` first reaches `post`/`take` already knowing the
//! answer, so making every such caller unwrap an `Optional` for the rare
//! refusal is the wrong trade. `post` answers `Ok(claim)`, `take` answers
//! `Ok((msg, request))`, and a transient refusal rides the error channel as
//! `Err(SosStatus.WouldBlock)`. A caller that genuinely polls declares itself
//! with a `match` carrying a `WouldBlock` arm. See `floor`'s level-decode
//! banner for where this diverges from `Channel.try_receive`.
//!
//! `resolve` does not poll. It consumes the claim and parks when the answer has
//! not arrived, so it is the waiter-free blocking receive. The question "has it
//! arrived" is `ready()`, which spends nothing and puts the terminal on the
//! error channel: `Err(PeerClosed)` means the reply can never come, so the
//! claim is dropped rather than resolved.
//!
//! All four kinds are §2.2 waitables, and the `Waiter` overloads at the bottom
//! of this file are how they attach. A caller chooses the door by whether it
//! wants to wait, never by what it will be told.
//!
//! There is no library type for a blocking send. §2.1's `send(msg)` and
//! `send(msg, timeout:)` are post + attach + wait, plus a Timer on the same
//! Waiter for the timeout: four calls a client writes inline out of the
//! primitives above (`tests/pipe-send-manual`). A wrapper type would freeze
//! that shape against the fused path (`PipeInlet.send`, one kernel call), and a
//! kernel-side timeout, if one is ever added, arrives as an op rather than as a
//! method on a wrapper.
//!
//! Single use is RAII here and a ledger in the kernel, and at the three
//! reply-side ops it is also a compile error. `reply`, `reply_wait` and
//! `resolve` consume the obligation or claim on every path they can take, so
//! they are `consumes` methods: the declaration carries the word in its effect
//! slot beside `&var self`, the call site spells `(move
//! obligation).reply(...)`, and a second use through the same binding is
//! refused by the compiler rather than diagnosed by the kernel. No sentinel is
//! written at any of them: a consuming body substitutes for the custom `deinit`
//! body, the handle word's synthesized drop is a no-op, and no release fires.
//! The kernel's ledger still stands behind each for a raw-altitude caller
//! holding a word.
//!
//! The transfer funnels that are not receivers, the `Waiter.give` overloads
//! below, keep the disarm, because `consumes` is a receiver-position effect and
//! they take their value as a by-value parameter.

import sosabi.{SosStatus, PipeInletHandle, PipeInletOp, PipeOutletHandle,
               PipeOutletOp, PipeReplyHandle, PipeRequestHandle, MINT_OP,
               NO_HANDLE, PIPE_BODY_BYTES, PIPE_MSG_HANDLES,
               BootHandleKind, InletLevel, OutletLevel, ReplyLevel,
               MemoryHandle, IoMemoryHandle, EventHandle, ThreadHandle,
               TimerHandle,
               PipeMsgMeta, PipeMsgRecord, PipeSendRecord, PipeReplyWaitRecord,
               PipeTakeRecord,
               ReplyWaitLeg, WaitBuffer, WaitTag, WaiterHandle,
               WAIT_HEADER_WORDS, WAIT_KEY_WORD, WAIT_TAG_WORD,
               WAIT_PAYLOAD_WORD, WAIT_HANDLE_SLOTS, WAIT_REQUEST_SLOT,
               WAIT_MSG_SLOT_0,
               pipe_msg_record_bytes, pipe_reply_wait_record_bytes,
               pipe_send_record_bytes, pipe_take_record_bytes,
               wait_record_bytes}

import sos.floor.{ALL_RIGHTS, call_value, checked, polled_ok,
                  sos_handle_release, sos_pipe_inlet_call, sos_pipe_inlet_post,
                  sos_pipe_outlet_take, sos_pipe_reply_ready,
                  sos_pipe_reply_resolve,
                  sos_pipe_request_reply, sos_pipe_request_reply_wait,
                  sos_waiter_wait}
import sos.waiter.{AttachMode, Waiter, mode_flag}
import sos.memory.{Memory}
import sos.iomemory.{IoMemory}
import sos.event.{Event}
import sos.timer.{Timer}
// Qualified, as in `sos.system`: `std.task` publishes a prelude `Thread<T>`,
// and a bare imported `Thread` ties with it.
import sos.thread

// =============================================================================
// The message vocabulary (spec §2.1's `send(data?, handles?)`)
// =============================================================================
//
// It lives here because a message can carry a capability, and a capability at
// this tier is an owning wrapper. `sos` depends on `sosabi` and nothing depends
// the other way (which is why op numbers can be kernel-internal), so the
// decoded forms (`PipeMsg`, `WaitResult`, `WaitPayload`, `decode_wait` and
// `Waiter.wait`) live in the highest module that can name every wrapper a
// message may carry.
//
// What stays in `sosabi` is the wire: the tag, the level enums, the record
// layouts and their offsets.

/// A handle that arrived in a message, typed at the boundary: the
/// `decode_boot_handle` precedent generalized.
///
/// Each case owns a wrapper, which makes a delivered capability the same kind
/// of thing as one that arrived any other way: it is `NoCopy`, its drop is a
/// release, and the no-forged-handles rule holds. A sender builds one by moving
/// a wrapper in and a receiver matches it out.
///
/// The set is "givable and declarable". Givable, because staging checks the
/// universal `Transfer` bit: Interrupt, Clock and Mapping handles never carry
/// it, so a case for one would be dead surface. Declarable,
/// because `Process` and `System` are declared in `sos.system`, which imports
/// this module, so a case for either would be an import cycle. The kernel
/// refuses to stage those two rather than deliver something the receiver could
/// not be told the name of; see `msg_kind_of`.
///
/// Take it out of its slot before you match it. `msg.handles[i].take()` hands
/// over an owned local, and a `match` on an owned local consumes it, which lets
/// an arm move the wrapper into a consuming op. Matching the slot in place is a
/// borrow, and a `move` out of a borrowing arm is a compiler soundness hole.
public enum PipeHandle {
    case Memory(m: Memory),
    case IoMemory(io: IoMemory),
    case Inlet(i: PipeInlet),
    case Outlet(o: PipeOutlet),
    case Reply(r: PipeReply),
    case Request(rq: PipeRequest),
    case Event(ev: Event),
    case Thread(t: thread.Thread),
    case Waiter(w: Waiter),
    case Timer(tm: Timer),
}

extension PipeHandle: NoCopy {}

/// The handles a message is being sent with: the send side's owning carrier.
///
/// It is not `PipeHandle`, for a language reason: a wrapper held inside an enum
/// payload can never be disarmed, because a match-arm binding is immutable
/// under every spelling (`&var self` included), so the transfer-funnel contract
/// `Process.give` runs on cannot be written. The send side therefore takes the
/// wrapper at the door, disarms it there, and keeps the kind and the word, as
/// `Process.give`'s funnels do, one overload per givable kind.
///
/// It is `NoCopy` and its drop releases what it still holds. A carrier that is
/// built and never sent releases its handles, as the wrappers would have.
///
/// A send either keeps the carrier's handles or consumes them. A transient
/// refusal (`WouldBlock`, `NoResource`, `QuotaExceeded`) leaves the slots
/// alone, so the caller may retry with the same carrier or drop it:
/// `Err(WouldBlock)` must not cost a caller its capabilities. Every other
/// outcome clears the slots: the kernel has taken the entries out of this
/// process's table, to deliver them or, on a terminal refusal such as
/// `PeerClosed`, to release them, so the drop is a no-op. Only `post_with` can
/// refuse the send itself transiently; the other sending ops clear the carrier
/// whatever they answer, because a fused op's `NoResource` or `QuotaExceeded`
/// at the reply's delivery comes after the carried handles were staged. See
/// `handed_over`.
public struct PipeHandles {
    public(package) kinds: [UInt8; PIPE_MSG_HANDLES],
    public(package) words: [UInt; PIPE_MSG_HANDLES],
}

extension PipeHandles: NoCopy {
    /// Release whatever is still here. A posted carrier is empty by then, so
    /// this is the un-posted path's cleanup and nothing else.
    func deinit(&var self) {
        var i: Int = 0
        while i < PIPE_MSG_HANDLES {
            if self.words[i] != NO_HANDLE {
                let _ = sos_handle_release(self.words[i])
            }
            i = i + 1
        }
    }
}

extension PipeHandles {
    /// A carrier with every slot empty: what a message with no handles sends,
    /// and the default every sending method takes.
    public static func none() -> PipeHandles {
        PipeHandles(kinds: [0; PIPE_MSG_HANDLES],
                    words: [NO_HANDLE; PIPE_MSG_HANDLES])
    }

    /// How many slots there are. Published so a caller can loop rather than
    /// spell `PIPE_MSG_HANDLES` (which it may; it is on the facade).
    public func slots(&self) -> Int { PIPE_MSG_HANDLES }

    /// Put a handle in slot `at`, consuming the wrapper. There is one overload
    /// per givable kind, in `Process.give`'s shape and for its reason: the
    /// funnel reads the wrapper's handle field and disarms it, which is only
    /// writable where the wrapper is a struct rather than an enum payload.
    ///
    /// An occupied slot is overwritten and the handle that was there is
    /// released, as it would have been had the caller dropped the wrapper. `at`
    /// outside the slot range panics: the count is published, so the caller
    /// could have checked.
    public func put(&var self, at: Int, memory: Memory) {
        self.free_slot(at)
        var owned = move memory
        self.kinds[at] = BootHandleKind.Memory as UInt8
        self.words[at] = owned.handle
        owned.handle = MemoryHandle(NO_HANDLE)
    }

    public func put(&var self, at: Int, iomemory: IoMemory) {
        self.free_slot(at)
        var owned = move iomemory
        self.kinds[at] = BootHandleKind.IoMemory as UInt8
        self.words[at] = owned.handle
        owned.handle = IoMemoryHandle(NO_HANDLE)
    }

    public func put(&var self, at: Int, inlet: PipeInlet) {
        self.free_slot(at)
        var owned = move inlet
        self.kinds[at] = BootHandleKind.PipeInlet as UInt8
        self.words[at] = owned.handle
        owned.handle = PipeInletHandle(NO_HANDLE)
    }

    public func put(&var self, at: Int, outlet: PipeOutlet) {
        self.free_slot(at)
        var owned = move outlet
        self.kinds[at] = BootHandleKind.PipeOutlet as UInt8
        self.words[at] = owned.handle
        owned.handle = PipeOutletHandle(NO_HANDLE)
    }

    public func put(&var self, at: Int, reply: PipeReply) {
        self.free_slot(at)
        var owned = move reply
        self.kinds[at] = BootHandleKind.PipeReply as UInt8
        self.words[at] = owned.handle
        owned.handle = PipeReplyHandle(NO_HANDLE)
    }

    public func put(&var self, at: Int, request: PipeRequest) {
        self.free_slot(at)
        var owned = move request
        self.kinds[at] = BootHandleKind.PipeRequest as UInt8
        self.words[at] = owned.handle
        owned.handle = PipeRequestHandle(NO_HANDLE)
    }

    public func put(&var self, at: Int, event: Event) {
        self.free_slot(at)
        var owned = move event
        self.kinds[at] = BootHandleKind.Event as UInt8
        self.words[at] = owned.handle
        owned.handle = EventHandle(NO_HANDLE)
    }

    public func put(&var self, at: Int, thread: thread.Thread) {
        self.free_slot(at)
        var owned = move thread
        self.kinds[at] = BootHandleKind.Thread as UInt8
        self.words[at] = owned.handle
        owned.handle = ThreadHandle(NO_HANDLE)
    }

    public func put(&var self, at: Int, waiter: Waiter) {
        self.free_slot(at)
        var owned = move waiter
        self.kinds[at] = BootHandleKind.Waiter as UInt8
        self.words[at] = owned.handle
        owned.handle = WaiterHandle(NO_HANDLE)
    }

    public func put(&var self, at: Int, timer: Timer) {
        self.free_slot(at)
        var owned = move timer
        self.kinds[at] = BootHandleKind.Timer as UInt8
        self.words[at] = owned.handle
        owned.handle = TimerHandle(NO_HANDLE)
    }

    /// Empty slot `at`, releasing whatever was in it. The bounds check lives
    /// here, so the funnels above read as one line of intent each.
    func free_slot(&var self, at: Int) {
        if at < 0 || at >= PIPE_MSG_HANDLES {
            panic("PipeHandles.put: slot out of range: {} (slots {})", at,
                  PIPE_MSG_HANDLES)
        }
        if self.words[at] != NO_HANDLE {
            let _ = sos_handle_release(self.words[at])
            self.words[at] = NO_HANDLE
            self.kinds[at] = 0
        }
    }

    /// Forget every slot without releasing. The kernel has unbound each sent
    /// entry from this process's table by the time any of its callers runs
    /// this: `post_body` calls it once the message is staged or on
    /// `PeerClosed`, and `call_body`, `reply_body` and `reply_wait_body` call
    /// it on every answer. A word kept past this point is stale.
    func handed_over(&var self) {
        var i: Int = 0
        while i < PIPE_MSG_HANDLES {
            self.kinds[i] = 0
            self.words[i] = NO_HANDLE
            i = i + 1
        }
    }
}

/// A message: how many of its bytes are real, the handles it carried, and the
/// bytes.
///
/// Handles come before bytes, and the Saw struct mirrors the record's order
/// (declaration-order ABI). It is the same layout the kernel writes, read from
/// the other side.
///
/// `NoCopy`, for two reasons. The bytes are API discipline (`PIPE_BODY_BYTES`
/// of inline storage should not be duplicated by an accident of binding), and
/// the handles are ownership proper: each is a capability this process holds
/// exactly once.
///
/// Take a handle out with `msg.handles[i].take()`, which needs a `var` binding
/// of the message and hands back an owned `PipeHandle?`. `None` means the
/// sender left the slot empty, and nothing else: a handle put into a message
/// leaves the sender's table at the post, so there is no window in which one
/// can go stale and no way for a slot to arrive empty that the sender did not
/// choose.
public struct PipeMsg {
    public len: UInt,
    public handles: [PipeHandle?; PIPE_MSG_HANDLES],
    public bytes: [UInt8; PIPE_BODY_BYTES],
}

extension PipeMsg: NoCopy {}

/// The answer to one exchange, as a reply-ready delivery hands it over.
///
/// The bytes arrive with the news, which is what makes a multiplexed RPC post +
/// attach + wait: the wait is the resolve, and a client that takes this door
/// never calls `resolve` at all. `PeerClosed` is the peer-gone doctrine's
/// answer on this arm (the obligation was dropped, so no answer is coming) and
/// carries nothing.
public enum ReplyDelivery {
    case Data(msg: PipeMsg),
    case PeerClosed,
}

extension ReplyDelivery: NoCopy {}

extension ReplyDelivery {
    /// Whether an answer actually arrived.
    public func arrived(&self) -> Bool {
        match self {
            case Data(_) -> true,
            case PeerClosed -> false,
        }
    }

    /// How many of the answer's bytes are real; zero when no answer came.
    ///
    /// These readers exist so a caller need not nest a `match`, which is a
    /// soundness matter: a place-match arm binding may be borrowed onward
    /// (passed as a `&` argument, or used as a `&self` receiver, as these are),
    /// but making it the scrutinee of a second match materializes it and drops
    /// it at the arm's end, releasing whatever it owns while the record still
    /// owns it too. Inside these bodies `self` is a reference and the match is
    /// single-level, so the payload is reached where it sits.
    public func len(&self) -> UInt {
        match self {
            case Data(msg) -> msg.len,
            case PeerClosed -> 0,
        }
    }

    /// Byte `i` of the answer; zero when no answer came.
    public func byte(&self, i: Int) -> UInt8 {
        match self {
            case Data(msg) -> msg.bytes[i],
            case PeerClosed -> 0,
        }
    }
}

/// What became ready, decoded: one case per waitable kind, plus `Message`, an
/// outlet attachment whose delivery was the take.
///
/// The payloads own their wrappers. A `Message` delivery carries a message and
/// the obligation to answer it, and saying so in the case means a server cannot
/// reach one without the other and cannot reach either on an arm where neither
/// exists.
///
/// Extraction goes through `WaitResult.open`. A reader that only looks may
/// still match through the place: a presence test, a word payload, and a
/// `&self` accessor called on an arm binding are all borrows and cost no
/// ceremony. What a looking reader may not do is re-match the binding: making a
/// place-match arm binding the scrutinee of a second match materializes it and
/// drops it at the arm's end, with no `move` written anywhere.
/// `ReplyDelivery`'s accessors exist for that.
public enum WaitPayload {
    case Event(word: UInt),
    case Interrupt(line: UInt),
    case Timer(fires: UInt),
    case Process(status: UInt),
    /// The server end has something to take, or will never have anything again.
    case Readable(state: OutletLevel),
    /// The client end has room to post, or nobody is left to take.
    case Room(state: InletLevel),
    /// The answer to one exchange arrived, or never will. When it arrived, the
    /// message itself is here.
    case Reply(outcome: ReplyDelivery),
    /// The claim behind this obligation was dropped. There is nothing to say
    /// beyond the fact, which is why this case has no payload.
    case Abandoned,
    /// The delivery was the take: an outlet attachment made by
    /// `Waiter.give(outlet:)` handing over a message and the obligation to
    /// answer it, in one record, with the server never having called `take`.
    /// Both are payloads because a `Message` delivery always carries both: the
    /// kernel mints the obligation as part of the delivery, so a record with
    /// one and not the other is a kernel/`sos` skew and `decode_wait` panics on
    /// it rather than handing back a `None`.
    case Message(msg: PipeMsg, request: PipeRequest),
}

extension WaitPayload: NoCopy {}

/// `Waiter.wait`'s answer: the attacher's key and what fired.
///
/// Everything a delivery hands over lives in `what`'s payload, where the case
/// that names it is the proof it is there. See `WaitPayload`.
public struct WaitResult {
    /// The word supplied at `add`, handed back unread. A plain read: it is a
    /// word, so looking at it borrows and consumes nothing.
    public key: UInt,
    public what: WaitPayload,
}

extension WaitResult: NoCopy {}

extension WaitResult {
    /// Take the answer apart. Consumes the record and hands back its key and
    /// its owned payload, which is the form a `match` may move out of.
    ///
    /// It exists because a match arm may not move a payload out of a borrowed
    /// scrutinee. The compiler does not yet enforce that, but intends to refuse
    /// it, so writing the licensed spelling now costs nothing. A `consumes`
    /// method may move its own fields out, on an every-path-or-no-path rule,
    /// and this moves unconditionally.
    ///
    /// Callers write `let (key, what) = (move wake).open()` and match `what`. A
    /// reader that only inspects need not open at all: matching through the
    /// place borrows, which is what a presence test or a word payload wants.
    public func open(&var self) consumes -> (UInt, WaitPayload) {
        (move self.key, move self.what)
    }
}

/// One handle slot of a delivered record, typed.
///
/// An unknown kind panics, on `decode_wait`'s terms: the kernel wrote the byte
/// out of the same `BootHandleKind` declaration this function reads, so a value
/// that does not decode is a kernel/`sos` skew rather than data from an
/// untrusted source. `Process` and `System` panic for a sharper reason: the
/// kernel refuses to stage either, so a message carrying one means the two
/// halves disagree about which kinds a message may carry.
func decode_handle(kind: UInt8, word: UInt) -> PipeHandle? {
    if word == NO_HANDLE {
        return None
    }
    guard let what = BootHandleKind.from(raw: kind as UInt) else {
        panic("sos: kernel wrote message handle kind {}, which this build cannot name",
              kind as UInt)
    }
    match what {
        case Memory -> PipeHandle.Memory(m: Memory(handle: MemoryHandle(word))),
        case IoMemory -> PipeHandle.IoMemory(io: IoMemory(handle: IoMemoryHandle(word))),
        case PipeInlet -> PipeHandle.Inlet(i: PipeInlet(handle: PipeInletHandle(word))),
        case PipeOutlet -> PipeHandle.Outlet(o: PipeOutlet(handle: PipeOutletHandle(word))),
        case PipeReply -> PipeHandle.Reply(r: PipeReply(handle: PipeReplyHandle(word))),
        case PipeRequest -> PipeHandle.Request(rq: PipeRequest(handle: PipeRequestHandle(word))),
        case Event -> PipeHandle.Event(ev: Event(handle: EventHandle(word))),
        case Thread -> PipeHandle.Thread(t: thread.Thread(handle: ThreadHandle(word))),
        case Waiter -> PipeHandle.Waiter(w: Waiter(handle: WaiterHandle(word))),
        case Timer -> PipeHandle.Timer(tm: Timer(handle: TimerHandle(word))),
        case Process -> {
            panic("sos: a message carried a Process handle, which the kernel refuses to stage")
        },
        case System -> {
            panic("sos: a message carried a System handle, which the kernel refuses to stage")
        },
    }
}

/// Build a `PipeMsg` out of a delivered record's meta, handle words and bytes.
/// The one place a message crosses from words into wrappers.
func msg_of(meta: &PipeMsgMeta, words: &[UInt; PIPE_MSG_HANDLES],
            bytes: &[UInt8; PIPE_BODY_BYTES]) -> PipeMsg {
    var msg = PipeMsg(len: meta.len as UInt,
                      handles: [None, None, None, None],
                      bytes: bytes)
    var i: Int = 0
    while i < PIPE_MSG_HANDLES {
        msg.handles[i] = decode_handle(meta.kinds[i], words[i])
        i = i + 1
    }
    move msg
}

// `msg_of` spells its empty-slot array out element by element, because a repeat
// literal cannot build a NoCopy optional: `[v; N]` needs an element that copies
// for free, and a `PipeHandle?` is move-only. So that one literal tracks this
// number by hand, and this assertion catches it if the number moves.
static_assert(PIPE_MSG_HANDLES == 4,
              "msg_of's empty-slot literal is spelled out and tracks PIPE_MSG_HANDLES by hand")

/// Decode a record the kernel copied out into `WaitResult`.
///
/// An unknown tag panics, deliberately. Every other decode in this package
/// treats its input as data a process might legitimately be handed; this one
/// does not, because the kernel wrote it out of the same `WaitTag` declaration
/// this function reads. A tag that does not decode is a skew between the kernel
/// and the `sos` module it exports, a bug in the pair rather than a runtime
/// condition, and a bug the caller cannot act on should stop the program where
/// it happened rather than become an `Err` somebody maps to "unknown". The same
/// rule covers the payload's own sub-enums and the handle kinds.
///
/// `public(package)` because `PipeRequest.reply_wait` is a second door onto the
/// same delivery. One record, one decode: a second copy of this match would be
/// the one place a kernel/`sos` skew could hide.
public(package) func decode_wait(buf: &WaitBuffer) -> WaitResult {
    let key = buf.header[WAIT_KEY_WORD]
    let tag = buf.header[WAIT_TAG_WORD]
    let payload = buf.header[WAIT_PAYLOAD_WORD]
    guard let kind = WaitTag.from(raw: tag) else {
        panic("sos: kernel wrote wait tag {}, which this build cannot name", tag)
    }
    match kind {
        case Event -> WaitResult(key: key, what: WaitPayload.Event(word: payload)),
        case Interrupt -> WaitResult(key: key,
                                     what: WaitPayload.Interrupt(line: payload)),
        case Timer -> WaitResult(key: key,
                                 what: WaitPayload.Timer(fires: payload)),
        // A process ended, and the payload is §8's status word, the same bits
        // `Process.get_status` answers with, so a supervisor parses one thing
        // however it learned.
        case Process -> WaitResult(key: key,
                                   what: WaitPayload.Process(status: payload)),
        // The pipe tags.
        case Readable -> {
            guard let level = OutletLevel.from(raw: payload) else {
                panic("sos: kernel wrote outlet level {}, which this build cannot name",
                      payload)
            }
            WaitResult(key: key, what: WaitPayload.Readable(state: level))
        },
        case Room -> {
            guard let level = InletLevel.from(raw: payload) else {
                panic("sos: kernel wrote inlet level {}, which this build cannot name",
                      payload)
            }
            WaitResult(key: key, what: WaitPayload.Room(state: level))
        },
        case Reply -> {
            guard let level = ReplyLevel.from(raw: payload) else {
                panic("sos: kernel wrote reply level {}, which this build cannot name",
                      payload)
            }
            match level {
                case Data -> {
                    let got = wait_message(&buf)
                    WaitResult(key: key,
                               what: WaitPayload.Reply(outcome: ReplyDelivery.Data(msg: move got)))
                },
                case PeerClosed -> {
                    WaitResult(key: key,
                               what: WaitPayload.Reply(outcome: ReplyDelivery.PeerClosed))
                },
            }
        },
        // Nothing to decode, and that is the case rather than an omission: the
        // fact is the notification, so the payload word carries no information
        // and `WaitPayload.Abandoned` binds nothing.
        case Abandoned -> WaitResult(key: key, what: WaitPayload.Abandoned),
        // The delivery-as-take. The record carries the message and the
        // obligation the kernel minted at the delivery, so the server never
        // reaches for `take`.
        //
        // A missing obligation is a skew, not a condition, and it panics on
        // this function's own terms: the kernel mints the request as part of
        // the delivery (a `Message` record it could not mint into is never
        // written; the whole batch is refused instead), so an empty slot here
        // means the two halves disagree about what this tag carries.
        case Message -> {
            let word = buf.handles[WAIT_REQUEST_SLOT]
            if word == NO_HANDLE {
                panic("sos: kernel wrote a Message delivery with no obligation")
            }
            let got = wait_message(&buf)
            let obligation = PipeRequest(handle: PipeRequestHandle(word))
            WaitResult(key: key,
                       what: WaitPayload.Message(msg: move got,
                                                 request: move obligation))
        },
    }
}

/// The message half of a delivered wait record. Split out because two tags
/// reach it and the handle region is at one offset for both.
func wait_message(buf: &WaitBuffer) -> PipeMsg {
    var words: [UInt; PIPE_MSG_HANDLES] = [NO_HANDLE; PIPE_MSG_HANDLES]
    var i: Int = 0
    while i < PIPE_MSG_HANDLES {
        words[i] = buf.handles[WAIT_MSG_SLOT_0 + i]
        i = i + 1
    }
    msg_of(&buf.meta, &words, &buf.body)
}

extension Waiter {
    /// Block until an attached handle is ready, and answer with the attacher's
    /// key and what fired. Requires `WaiterRight.Wait`.
    ///
    /// It is declared here rather than in `sos.waiter` because of its return
    /// type: a delivery may hand over a message full of capabilities, so
    /// `WaitResult` names every wrapper a message can carry and therefore sits
    /// in this module. `Waiter.add(outlet:)` and `Waiter.add(process:)` are the
    /// same shape for the same kind of reason, and the facade's `public import`
    /// makes all of it one list at the call site.
    ///
    /// It returns immediately when something is already ready. Readiness is
    /// level-triggered, so the readiness a signal left behind is still there:
    /// "signal then wait" and "wait then signal" have the same outcome, and
    /// only the second parks.
    ///
    /// The answer consumes what it reports, for an Event and a Timer alike: the
    /// record carries the accumulated word (or the fire count) and the object
    /// is emptied in the same step, so nothing is reported twice. An Interrupt
    /// is the exception (§9): its readiness ends at `ack`.
    ///
    /// Several threads on one Waiter is a distribution: a ready handle wakes
    /// one of them and that thread's record carries the value, so a worker pool
    /// shares a stream of notifications with no coordination.
    ///
    /// A park can end in `Err(SosStatus.Revoked)`: if this Waiter's last handle
    /// is released while you are parked on it, the kernel wakes you with that
    /// status rather than leaving you blocked forever. No record is written on
    /// that path.
    ///
    /// It can also end in `Err(NoResource)` or `Err(QuotaExceeded)`. A delivery
    /// that hands over handles has to mint them into this process's table, and
    /// a full table delivers nothing: the message stays staged, its level stays
    /// raised, and waiting again after freeing a slot is handed the same
    /// message. It is a resource condition rather than a bug, which is why it
    /// is a status.
    ///
    /// The buffer is this frame's, and a caller never sees it. It is max-sized
    /// and the copy is not: a parked waiter cannot predict which arm fires, so
    /// the buffer holds the biggest answer its attachments could give, while
    /// the kernel writes only what the delivery needs.
    public func wait(&self) -> Result<WaitResult, SosStatus> {
        wait_into_frame(self.handle)
    }
}

/// The `unsafe` half of `wait`, confined to one small named helper. The
/// buffer's address never leaves it.
func wait_into_frame(handle: WaiterHandle) unsafe -> Result<WaitResult, SosStatus> {
    var buf = WaitBuffer(header: [0; WAIT_HEADER_WORDS],
                         meta: PipeMsgMeta(len: 0, kinds: [0; PIPE_MSG_HANDLES]),
                         handles: [NO_HANDLE; WAIT_HANDLE_SLOTS],
                         body: [0; PIPE_BODY_BYTES])
    try checked(sos_waiter_wait(handle,
                                (&var buf.header) as UnsafePointer<UInt>,
                                wait_record_bytes() as UInt))
    return decode_wait(&buf)
}

/// A handle to a PipeInlet, the client end of a connection, where requests flow
/// in (spec §2.1).
///
/// A role is an object, not a rights mask. The kernel does not carry one
/// symmetric pipe object with read and write rights on it, because a parked
/// receiver is itself a holder, and a write-capable count on such a thing could
/// never reach zero (the socket-`shutdown()` hole). The bidirectionality
/// dissolves instead: holding one of these means you are the client, and "the
/// writers went to zero" is "every PipeInlet handle onto this connection is
/// gone", which the ordinary reference ledger answers.
///
/// Built by `Process.pipe_create`, `mint`, `BootHandle.take_inlet`, a
/// message's handle decode, and a refused `Waiter.give(inlet:)` handing it
/// back: no word constructor, no word extractor.
///
/// `NoCopy`, and dropping it releases the handle. Here that matters beyond
/// tidiness: dropping the last inlet is what tells the far end that no request
/// will ever arrive again. Release is the only way to say it, and RAII makes
/// saying it the default. `let _ = move inlet` is a deliberate close, and
/// letting one fall out of scope is the same act written by the language.
public struct PipeInlet {
    public(package) handle: PipeInletHandle,
}

extension PipeInlet: NoCopy {
    /// Drop is release; see `System`'s deinit for the sentinel and the discard.
    func deinit(&var self) {
        if self.handle != NO_HANDLE {
            let _ = sos_handle_release(self.handle)
        }
    }
}

extension PipeInlet {
    /// Stage a message for the far end. Requires `PipeInletRight.Post`.
    ///
    /// `Ok(claim)`: the message is staged. `Err(SosStatus.WouldBlock)`: the
    /// ring is full and nothing was staged; call again once an exchange has
    /// settled. The transient rides the error channel so a poll loop can tell
    /// "not yet" from "broken" with a `match` carrying a `WouldBlock` arm, at
    /// no cost to the callers that reach this op knowing there is room. A
    /// caller at its handle quota gets `Err(SosStatus.QuotaExceeded)` and a
    /// full handle table `Err(SosStatus.NoResource)`: the claim could not be
    /// minted, and nothing was staged.
    ///
    /// It never blocks. The client side has two kernel primitives, this and
    /// the fused `send`; `send(timeout:)`, tell and cancel are compositions
    /// over `post` plus the Timer and Waiter machinery, which keeps the kernel
    /// from ever learning what a timeout is. The pieces they compose out of
    /// (post, the attaches, the wait) are all in this file, and the
    /// composition itself is the caller's, written inline.
    ///
    /// `len` may be zero (a data-free message is §2.1's own option), and a
    /// zero-length post reads no buffer at all. A `len` above `PIPE_BODY_BYTES`
    /// terminates the caller: the maximum is published, and the caller chose
    /// the number.
    ///
    /// `Err(SosStatus.PeerClosed)` means every outlet handle onto this
    /// connection is gone, so nobody can ever take what you would send. It is
    /// terminal, since nothing un-closes, so a caller may stop rather than
    /// retry. A message that carried handles has them released.
    ///
    /// It answers the claim (§2.1's `send` yields a `PipeReplyHandle`, and this
    /// is it). `Ok(claim)` is a staged message and the client's claim on the
    /// one reply it is owed. Hold it and `resolve` it, hand it on with `give`,
    /// or drop it. Dropping is §2.1's cancellation primitive and the tell
    /// idiom's second half: the server is told by its `reply` answering
    /// `PeerClosed`.
    ///
    /// A claim holds a ring slot until the exchange settles. A full ring
    /// therefore counts awaiting-reply exchanges, not merely untaken messages,
    /// so a client that never resolves will meet its own in-flight budget.
    public func post(&self, body: &[UInt8; PIPE_BODY_BYTES],
                     len: UInt) -> Result<PipeReply, SosStatus> {
        var none = PipeHandles.none()
        return post_body(self.handle, &body, len, &var none)
    }

    /// The same post, carrying handles (spec §2.1's `send(data?, handles?)`).
    ///
    /// This is a separate name from `post`, not an overload. A bare integer
    /// literal stops adopting a `UInt` parameter once a method has two
    /// candidates, so `post(body:, len: 1)` would need an `as UInt` at every
    /// call site that passes a literal. The `_with` verbs can collapse into
    /// overloads with no call-site change once the compiler adopts literals
    /// across overloads.
    ///
    /// It is also not a defaulted parameter, and the carrier is `&var` rather
    /// than by value, because a refused post must not cost a caller its
    /// capabilities: `Err(WouldBlock)` is a full ring and the ordinary thing a
    /// poll loop meets, so the handles have to still be there to try again
    /// with.
    ///
    /// The carrier's fate follows the status. A transient refusal
    /// (`WouldBlock`, `NoResource` when the table has no room for the claim,
    /// `QuotaExceeded` when the handle budget is spent) leaves the carrier
    /// untouched, with every wrapper live. A staged message empties it, and so
    /// does `Err(PeerClosed)`: that refusal is terminal, so the kernel releases
    /// the carried handles. Releasing may destroy the object, when the sent
    /// handle was its last: the far end of a carried pipe end is told, for
    /// example.
    ///
    /// The entries leave this process's table at the post: the kernel unbinds
    /// each sent handle and holds it in the exchange until the receiver's
    /// take, or a delivery, mints it into the receiver's table. A copy of a
    /// sent word kept anywhere is stale from the post on, and using it, a
    /// release included, is a `BadHandle` fault that ends the process. A
    /// transient refusal takes nothing, and `PeerClosed` takes them to release
    /// them.
    ///
    /// The carrier cannot hold an outlet of this inlet's own connection (the
    /// outlet `pipe_create` returned beside it, or one of its siblings): the
    /// staged reference would keep the connection from ever being freed, so
    /// the kernel terminates the caller with a `BadArg` fault.
    public func post_with(&self, body: &[UInt8; PIPE_BODY_BYTES], len: UInt,
                          handles: &var PipeHandles) -> Result<PipeReply, SosStatus> {
        post_body(self.handle, &body, len, &var handles)
    }

    /// Send a message and wait for its answer in one trap: §2.1's `send`.
    /// Requires `PipeInletRight.Post`, the same bit `post` spends.
    ///
    /// This is the fused `Call`, and that is why it is a method at all. A
    /// library `send` written over post + attach + wait would be a wrapper type
    /// that freezes a shape which cannot later be refactored into a simple
    /// kernel call. This one is the simple kernel call: one op, no Waiter, no
    /// attachment, no claim handle.
    ///
    /// Nothing is minted. A fused call's claim is kernel-internal and tied to
    /// this thread, so a blocking client holds no `PipeReply`: no table row, no
    /// quota, no RAII to get right, and no in-flight depth bounded by
    /// `MAX_HANDLES`.
    ///
    /// It blocks, and a full ring blocks too, never `WouldBlock`. The caller
    /// parks on the connection's room-to-post level and resumes when an
    /// exchange settles, so backpressure is a wait rather than a poll loop.
    ///
    /// There is no timeout argument: the kernel does not know what a timeout
    /// is. A caller that needs a deadline composes `post` + `add(reply:)` + a
    /// Timer on one Waiter (`tests/pipe-send-manual`).
    ///
    /// `len` may be zero (a data-free message is §2.1's own option). A `len`
    /// above `PIPE_BODY_BYTES` terminates the caller, as at a post.
    ///
    /// `Err(SosStatus.PeerClosed)` is `resolve`'s convention and covers every
    /// way an answer can fail to arrive: the server end was already gone, the
    /// server dropped the obligation, or the server end went while this thread
    /// was parked. It is terminal for this exchange; whether the connection has
    /// a future is what a later call finds out.
    ///
    /// `Err(SosStatus.NoResource)` or `Err(SosStatus.QuotaExceeded)` is a reply
    /// whose handles found no room in this process's table. The reply and its
    /// handles are lost, because a parked call cannot ask again; make table
    /// room before calling a server that answers with handles.
    public func send(&self, body: &[UInt8; PIPE_BODY_BYTES],
                     len: UInt) -> Result<PipeMsg, SosStatus> {
        var none = PipeHandles.none()
        return call_body(self.handle, &body, len, &var none)
    }

    /// The same call, carrying handles. Everything `post_with` says about when
    /// the entries leave holds, with one difference the fused path makes: a
    /// `Call` never refuses the send itself transiently (a full ring parks it),
    /// so the carrier is emptied on every path that returns. A `NoResource` or
    /// `QuotaExceeded` answered at the reply's delivery comes after the message
    /// was staged, so it consumes the handles with it. `Err(PeerClosed)`
    /// consumes the handles too,
    /// whether or not the message was staged: when the server end was gone
    /// before the message was staged, or went while the call waited for room,
    /// the kernel releases them, and that may destroy an object whose last
    /// handle this was. The answer may carry handles too, because §2.1's
    /// `send(data?, handles?)` is symmetric: the `PipeMsg` that comes back has
    /// slots of its own. As at `post_with`, an outlet of this inlet's own
    /// connection in the carrier terminates the caller with a `BadArg` fault.
    public func send_with(&self, body: &[UInt8; PIPE_BODY_BYTES], len: UInt,
                          handles: &var PipeHandles) -> Result<PipeMsg, SosStatus> {
        call_body(self.handle, &body, len, &var handles)
    }

    /// Mint a sibling of this inlet, narrowed by a keep mask: the universal
    /// `MINT_OP`, on `Memory.mint`'s exact terms. Requires the universal
    /// `PipeInletRight.Mint` on this handle.
    ///
    /// This is where a launcher's pipe policy is written. An endpoint's default
    /// set is permissive (`Transfer | Mint | Post | Wait`; attenuation is
    /// monotonic, so a bit not minted at creation could never appear later), so
    /// what a child may do with the end it is handed is decided here, once, by
    /// the mask:
    ///
    /// ```saw
    /// let for_child = inlet.mint(rights: PipeInletRight.Transfer
    ///                                    | PipeInletRight.Post)!
    /// child.give(inlet: move for_child, tag: REQUESTS)
    /// ```
    ///
    /// That child can post and can hand the end on, and can neither attach the
    /// end to a Waiter nor mint a sibling of its own.
    ///
    /// A sibling is a second name and therefore a second reference on this
    /// connection's inlet column, so any handle to an end holds that end open,
    /// by construction (the Unix `dup` precedent). Dropping one of two siblings
    /// tells the far end nothing; only the last one going is news.
    public func mint(&self,
                     rights: UInt32 = ALL_RIGHTS) -> Result<PipeInlet, SosStatus> {
        let raw = try call_value(self.handle, MINT_OP, rights as UInt, 0, 0)
        return PipeInlet(handle: PipeInletHandle(raw))
    }
}

/// The `unsafe` half of `PipeInlet.post`, confined to one small named helper.
/// The body's address never leaves it.
///
/// The word the kernel answers with is a handle, and this is the one place it
/// is ever seen as a number: the wrapper is built here and everything above
/// this line owns a `PipeReply` instead.
func post_body(handle: PipeInletHandle, body: &[UInt8; PIPE_BODY_BYTES],
               len: UInt,
               handles: &var PipeHandles) unsafe -> Result<PipeReply, SosStatus> {
    var staged: [UInt64; PIPE_STAGE_DWORDS] = [0; PIPE_STAGE_DWORDS]
    var args = send_record(body_addr_for_kernel(&body, &var staged), len, &handles)
    var value: UInt = 0
    let raw = sos_pipe_inlet_post(handle,
                                  (&var args) as UnsafePointer<UInt>,
                                  pipe_send_record_bytes() as UInt,
                                  (&var value) as UnsafePointer<UInt>)
    // `checked` decodes the answer: the wire still says `WouldBlock`, and the
    // typed answer puts it on the error channel.
    //
    // The status is read before the carrier is touched. A transient refusal
    // (`WouldBlock`, `NoResource`, `QuotaExceeded`) leaves the entries bound,
    // so the carrier keeps its wrappers and the caller may retry with it. Any
    // other status is terminal: the kernel has released the entries, so the
    // words are stale. The set is closed by name so that a status added later
    // empties the carrier, which leaks at worst, rather than keeps stale words
    // that fault when the carrier drops.
    try checked(raw) catch {
        match error {
            case WouldBlock -> {},
            case NoResource -> {},
            case QuotaExceeded -> {},
            case _ -> { handles.handed_over() },
        }
        return error
    }
    // A staged message: the kernel has unbound the sent entries and holds them
    // for the receiver, so the words are stale and releasing one here would be
    // a `BadHandle` fault.
    handles.handed_over()
    return PipeReply(handle: PipeReplyHandle(value))
}

/// The staging buffer's length, in doublewords. It is `UInt64` rather than the
/// machine word, on purpose: a `[UInt64; N]` local is eight-aligned by the
/// natural ABI on both targets, which satisfies any machine-word alignment the
/// copy doors could ask, and its count is one literal instead of a per-target
/// `sizeof` division.
static PIPE_STAGE_DWORDS: Int = 16

static_assert(PIPE_STAGE_DWORDS * 8 == PIPE_BODY_BYTES,
              "the staging buffer must cover exactly one pipe body")

/// The address the kernel may read the body from: the caller's own storage
/// where it is already word-aligned, a copy in `staged` where it is not.
///
/// The copy-in door faults a source that is not word-aligned (the rule
/// `kernel/core/process.saw` states once for every kernel record), and the
/// public surface takes `&[UInt8; PIPE_BODY_BYTES]`, a byte array whose
/// alignment is one. Saw has no alignment attribute, so every caller's `var
/// body: [UInt8; ...]` local is word-aligned only by the accident of stack
/// layout, which an optimization level can change. The contract is made to
/// hold here, for every caller ever written, rather than re-learned at each
/// call site. The aligned common case pays one branch and no
/// copy; an unaligned caller pays a `PIPE_BODY_BYTES` copy it cannot notice
/// next to the trap it is about to take. `staged` is the funnel's frame-local,
/// so it outlives the syscall that reads it.
func body_addr_for_kernel(body: &[UInt8; PIPE_BODY_BYTES],
                          staged: &var [UInt64; PIPE_STAGE_DWORDS]) unsafe -> UInt {
    let src = (&body) as UnsafePointer<UInt8>
    let addr = src as UInt
    if addr % (sizeof<UInt>() as UInt) == 0 {
        return addr
    }
    let dst = (&var staged) as UnsafePointer<UInt8>
    var i: Int = 0
    while i < PIPE_BODY_BYTES {
        dst[i] = src[i]
        i = i + 1
    }
    dst as UInt
}

/// Assemble the argument record every sending op takes: a body address, a
/// length and the handle words.
///
/// The address arrives already vetted through `body_addr_for_kernel`. The
/// funnel owns the staging storage, because the record only carries the number
/// and the kernel reads through it after this function has returned.
///
/// It reads the carrier and does not empty it, which lets each caller decide
/// per answer whether the handles were handed over. The kinds are not sent: the
/// kernel reads each word out of this process's own table, so it knows the kind
/// better than the caller could tell it, and a kind argument would be a second
/// description of one fact.
func send_record(body_addr: UInt, len: UInt,
                 handles: &PipeHandles) unsafe -> PipeSendRecord {
    var args = PipeSendRecord(body: body_addr,
                              len: len,
                              handles: [NO_HANDLE; PIPE_MSG_HANDLES])
    var i: Int = 0
    while i < PIPE_MSG_HANDLES {
        args.handles[i] = handles.words[i]
        i = i + 1
    }
    args
}

/// The `unsafe` half of `PipeInlet.send`, confined to one small named helper.
/// Neither address leaves it.
///
/// The reply buffer is this frame's, as `Waiter.wait`'s record buffer is, for
/// the same reason: the kernel answers into the caller's own memory, so
/// somebody has to supply the bytes, and supplying them here keeps a raw
/// address out of the typed surface. It is a whole slot wide because a caller
/// cannot size a buffer against a length it has not been told yet (the
/// published-minimum rule).
///
/// A fused call has no would-block outcome at all, since a full ring parks it,
/// which distinguishes it from `post` beside it, though both decode through
/// `checked`.
func call_body(handle: PipeInletHandle, body: &[UInt8; PIPE_BODY_BYTES],
               len: UInt,
               handles: &var PipeHandles) unsafe -> Result<PipeMsg, SosStatus> {
    var staged: [UInt64; PIPE_STAGE_DWORDS] = [0; PIPE_STAGE_DWORDS]
    var args = send_record(body_addr_for_kernel(&body, &var staged), len, &handles)
    var reply = PipeMsgRecord(meta: PipeMsgMeta(len: 0,
                                                kinds: [0; PIPE_MSG_HANDLES]),
                              handles: [NO_HANDLE; PIPE_MSG_HANDLES],
                              bytes: [0; PIPE_BODY_BYTES])
    var value: UInt = 0
    let raw = sos_pipe_inlet_call(handle,
                                  (&var args) as UnsafePointer<UInt>,
                                  pipe_send_record_bytes() as UInt,
                                  (&var reply) as UnsafePointer<UInt>,
                                  (&var value) as UnsafePointer<UInt>)
    // The carrier is emptied before the answer is read, unlike `post`'s: a
    // `Call` parks rather than refusing the send, so every answer consumes the
    // entries. A staged message carries them to the server, a `PeerClosed`
    // from a server end that was gone before or during the park has the kernel
    // release them, and a `NoResource` or `QuotaExceeded` at the reply's
    // delivery comes after they were staged.
    handles.handed_over()
    try checked(raw)
    return msg_of(&reply.meta, &reply.handles, &reply.bytes)
}

/// A handle to a PipeOutlet, the server end of a connection, where requests
/// come out (spec §2.1).
///
/// The mirror of `PipeInlet` in every respect but two: which reference column
/// it counts on, and which op it carries. What that type's docstring says about
/// roles-as-objects, `NoCopy` and what dropping the last one means holds here
/// with the direction reversed: a dropped last outlet tells the client that
/// nothing it sends can ever be read, the same peer-gone doctrine seen from the
/// other side.
///
/// Built by `Process.pipe_create`, `mint`, `BootHandle.take_outlet`, a
/// message's handle decode, and a refused `Waiter.give(outlet:)` handing it
/// back.
public struct PipeOutlet {
    public(package) handle: PipeOutletHandle,
}

extension PipeOutlet: NoCopy {
    /// Drop is release; see `System`'s deinit for the sentinel and the discard.
    func deinit(&var self) {
        if self.handle != NO_HANDLE {
            let _ = sos_handle_release(self.handle)
        }
    }
}

extension PipeOutlet {
    /// Take the oldest staged message, and answer it with the obligation to
    /// reply to it. Requires `PipeOutletRight.Take`.
    ///
    /// `Ok((msg, request))`: a message arrived and `request` is the server's
    /// obligation to send exactly one reply. `Err(SosStatus.WouldBlock)`:
    /// nothing is staged; call again. The transient rides the error channel: a
    /// server polling an idle connection is not a server meeting a failure, and
    /// a `match` with a `WouldBlock` arm says so out loud, where every other
    /// caller of this op (one that waited on the readable level first) need not
    /// unwrap anything.
    ///
    /// Discharge the obligation with `reply`, hand it on with `give` (§2.1's
    /// delegation primitive; the reply then goes straight to the original
    /// client), or drop it, which tells the client `PeerClosed` at its next
    /// `resolve`. Dropping is a legitimate answer; forgetting is not available,
    /// because the ring slot behind it is held until the exchange settles.
    ///
    /// `msg.len` may be zero, and that is a message rather than an absence:
    /// §2.1 makes a data-free message an option, so the two are different
    /// answers and the two channels keep them apart.
    ///
    /// The record buffer is this frame's and a whole slot wide, not a
    /// message's: a caller cannot size a buffer against a length it has not
    /// been told yet, the same published-minimum rule `Waiter.wait`'s record
    /// follows.
    ///
    /// A caller at its handle quota gets `Err(SosStatus.QuotaExceeded)` and a
    /// full handle table `Err(SosStatus.NoResource)`: the obligation and the
    /// handles the message carries could not all be minted. Nothing is taken,
    /// so a later take after freeing room is handed the same message.
    ///
    /// It never parks, and does not have to: an outlet is a §2.2 waitable, so a
    /// server attaches it (`Waiter.add(outlet:)`), waits for the readable
    /// level, and calls this once it knows there is something. That is a
    /// blocking receive composed out of two ops.
    ///
    /// `Err(SosStatus.PeerClosed)` means the ring is empty and every inlet
    /// handle is gone. The order is the contract, and it is drain-first:
    /// everything the departed client staged is handed over before this is ever
    /// answered, so a sender exiting mid-conversation loses nothing. Afterwards
    /// it is terminal (no new sender can appear, because attenuation is
    /// monotonic and a column at zero has no handle left to mint from), so
    /// every later take answers the same.
    public func take(&self) -> Result<(PipeMsg, PipeRequest), SosStatus> {
        take_into_frame(self.handle)
    }

    /// Mint a sibling of this outlet, narrowed by a keep mask. Everything
    /// `PipeInlet.mint` says holds here with `Take` in `Post`'s place,
    /// including the part that matters for the connection: a sibling is a
    /// second reference on the outlet column, so a server that mints one and
    /// hands it to a helper has two names on one end and the client is told
    /// nothing until both are gone.
    public func mint(&self,
                     rights: UInt32 = ALL_RIGHTS) -> Result<PipeOutlet, SosStatus> {
        let raw = try call_value(self.handle, MINT_OP, rights as UInt, 0, 0)
        return PipeOutlet(handle: PipeOutletHandle(raw))
    }
}

/// The `unsafe` half of `PipeOutlet.take`, confined to one small named helper.
/// Neither address leaves it.
///
/// The tuple is the right answer, on `Process.pipe_create`'s reasoning: a take
/// that succeeds always carries exactly two things and the caller always wants
/// both, so a destructuring `let (msg, request) = try ...` moves each into its
/// own binding with no husk left behind. A `BootHandle` needs an `Optional` per
/// slot because it carries at most one of several kinds and asking the wrong
/// question must be harmless.
func take_into_frame(
    handle: PipeOutletHandle
) unsafe -> Result<(PipeMsg, PipeRequest), SosStatus> {
    var rec = PipeTakeRecord(
        request: NO_HANDLE,
        msg: PipeMsgRecord(meta: PipeMsgMeta(len: 0,
                                             kinds: [0; PIPE_MSG_HANDLES]),
                           handles: [NO_HANDLE; PIPE_MSG_HANDLES],
                           bytes: [0; PIPE_BODY_BYTES]))
    let raw = sos_pipe_outlet_take(handle,
                                   (&var rec) as UnsafePointer<UInt>,
                                   pipe_take_record_bytes() as UInt)
    // `checked` decodes the answer: the wire word is the same one a poll decode
    // would read, and the typed answer puts `WouldBlock` on the error channel.
    try checked(raw)
    let request = PipeRequest(handle: PipeRequestHandle(rec.request))
    let got: (PipeMsg, PipeRequest) = (msg_of(&rec.msg.meta, &rec.msg.handles,
                                              &rec.msg.bytes),
                                       move request)
    return move got
}

/// A handle to a PipeReply, the client's claim on one reply (spec §2.1's
/// `PipeReplyHandle`).
///
/// It is what a post answers with. Holding it is what makes request/reply a
/// primitive rather than a convention: the reply capability is unforgeable,
/// single-use, and cannot be confused with any other exchange on the same
/// connection, because it names a ring slot rather than a pipe.
///
/// `NoCopy`, and dropping it is the cancellation primitive (§2.1). There is no
/// `cancel()` verb: a client that has given up releases the claim, and the
/// server learns of it when its own `reply` answers `PeerClosed`. Abandonment
/// is information, not an imperative: whether it cancels anything is the
/// protocol's decision, and a tell-style message ignores the signal entirely.
/// Deterministic destruction makes the timing of the signal deterministic too.
///
/// Dropping is also what `ready()`'s terminal asks for: a claim whose exchange
/// can never be answered is released, not resolved.
///
/// Built by `PipeInlet.post` and `PipeInlet.post_with`, a boot record's
/// decode (`BootHandle.take_reply`), a message's handle decode, and a refused
/// `Waiter.give(reply:)` handing it back: no word constructor, no word
/// extractor.
public struct PipeReply {
    public(package) handle: PipeReplyHandle,
}

extension PipeReply: NoCopy {
    /// Drop is release; see `System`'s deinit for the sentinel and the discard.
    /// A resolved claim never reaches this body: `resolve` is a `consumes`
    /// method, so its consuming body stands in for this one on that path and
    /// there is no husk and no sentinel to check. What is left here is a claim
    /// the holder chose not to spend.
    func deinit(&var self) {
        if self.handle != NO_HANDLE {
            let _ = sos_handle_release(self.handle)
        }
    }
}

extension PipeReply {
    /// Has the answer arrived? The level, asked without spending anything.
    /// Requires `PipeReplyRight.Wait`, the bit that gates attaching this claim,
    /// because it is the same question asked two ways; `Resolve` stays the
    /// consuming act's authority.
    ///
    /// The three answers:
    ///
    ///   - `Ok(true)`: the reply has arrived. `(move claim).resolve()` will
    ///     return it without parking.
    ///   - `Ok(false)`: not yet, and the connection is still good. Ask again,
    ///     or resolve and park, or attach and wait.
    ///   - `Err(SosStatus.PeerClosed)`: the reply can never come. The terminal
    ///     is on the error channel so that there is no second call to make: the
    ///     caller drops the claim (the wrapper's ordinary release) rather than
    ///     spending a resolve on a dead exchange.
    ///
    /// Readiness is monotonic, so ready-then-resolve has no check-then-act
    /// race: an exchange goes from pending to settled and never back, and the
    /// only way a `true` could become anything else is somebody else settling
    /// the slot, which cannot happen because a claim has one name and this one
    /// is yours.
    ///
    /// It borrows rather than consuming, which lets a poll loop call it as many
    /// times as it likes and then move the claim into the resolve.
    public func ready(&self) -> Result<Bool, SosStatus> {
        polled_ok(sos_pipe_reply_ready(self.handle))
    }

    /// Take the reply, parking until it is there, and spend the claim. Requires
    /// `PipeReplyRight.Resolve`.
    ///
    /// `Ok(msg)`: the reply is here and its bytes and handles are yours.
    /// `msg.len` may be zero, which is a reply that carries no data rather than
    /// an absence.
    ///
    /// It parks, and it is the waiter-free blocking receive. A pending exchange
    /// blocks the calling thread on the claim's ready level inside the kernel,
    /// so a client that wants exactly one answer needs no Waiter, no attachment
    /// and no poll loop, and `PipeInlet.send` remains the one-trap form for the
    /// whole round trip. The non-blocking question is `ready()`. A claim
    /// attached to a Waiter (`add(reply:)`) is a third door that uses neither:
    /// the answer is delivered in the wait record, and a `resolve` on a claim
    /// whose answer was delivered that way answers `PeerClosed`, because there
    /// is no second copy of an answer.
    ///
    /// `Err(SosStatus.PeerClosed)` means the server side abandoned it: the
    /// obligation was dropped without a reply, or the connection's server end
    /// went away before the message could ever be taken.
    ///
    /// It is a `consumes` method, and every path spends the claim. The call
    /// spells the move, `(move claim).resolve()`, so the binding is dead past
    /// that expression and a second resolve is a compile error rather than the
    /// kernel's `BadHandle`. Nothing is disarmed and nothing is released: the
    /// consuming body stands in for this type's `deinit` on this path, as
    /// `reply`'s does. The kernel's ledger still stands behind the word for a
    /// raw-altitude caller.
    ///
    /// A refused handle mint spends the claim too. A reply may carry
    /// capabilities, and a caller whose table has no room for them is answered
    /// `Err(NoResource)` or `Err(QuotaExceeded)` with the claim gone, because a
    /// consuming op leaves nothing to retry with. Make table room before
    /// resolving a reply you expect handles on; the fused `PipeInlet.send`
    /// makes the same trade for the same reason.
    public func resolve(&var self) consumes -> Result<PipeMsg, SosStatus> {
        resolve_into_frame(self.handle)
    }

    /// Mint a sibling of this claim, narrowed by a keep mask: the universal
    /// `MINT_OP`, on `PipeInlet.mint`'s exact terms, except that here it cannot
    /// succeed.
    ///
    /// A one-shot has one name. `pipe_reply_rights()` withholds the universal
    /// `Mint` bit, and attenuation is monotonic, so no `PipeReply` this kernel
    /// mints carries it, and calling this terminates the caller with
    /// `AccessDenied`. The method is kept rather than deleted because that
    /// refusal is the rule's witness (a sibling would be a second claim on an
    /// exchange only one act may spend, resolving into `PeerClosed` at best),
    /// and the tree proves rules by executing them (`tests/pipe-dead-claim`).
    /// Attenuate-then-give is `give`'s keep mask, which needs no sibling at
    /// all.
    public func mint(&self,
                     rights: UInt32 = ALL_RIGHTS) -> Result<PipeReply, SosStatus> {
        let raw = try call_value(self.handle, MINT_OP, rights as UInt, 0, 0)
        return PipeReply(handle: PipeReplyHandle(raw))
    }
}

/// The `unsafe` half of `PipeReply.resolve`, confined to one small named
/// helper. The buffer's address never leaves it.
///
/// There is no disarm here, and nothing takes its place: the caller is a
/// `consumes` method whose body is the wrapper's teardown on this path, the
/// handle word is swept by a synthesized drop that does nothing, and this
/// helper takes the word like every other one in the file.
func resolve_into_frame(handle: PipeReplyHandle) unsafe -> Result<PipeMsg, SosStatus> {
    var rec = PipeMsgRecord(meta: PipeMsgMeta(len: 0,
                                              kinds: [0; PIPE_MSG_HANDLES]),
                            handles: [NO_HANDLE; PIPE_MSG_HANDLES],
                            bytes: [0; PIPE_BODY_BYTES])
    var value: UInt = 0
    let raw = sos_pipe_reply_resolve(handle,
                                     (&var rec) as UnsafePointer<UInt>,
                                     pipe_msg_record_bytes() as UInt,
                                     (&var value) as UnsafePointer<UInt>)
    try checked(raw)
    return msg_of(&rec.meta, &rec.handles, &rec.bytes)
}

/// A handle to a PipeRequest, the server's obligation to send one reply (spec
/// §2.1's `PipeRequestHandle`).
///
/// It is what a take answers with beside the message, and it is why §2.1 calls
/// request/reply a primitive: the server does not have to know who asked, or
/// over which connection, or how to address an answer. It holds the obligation,
/// and the obligation knows.
///
/// It is the delegation primitive (§2.1): `Transfer` is minted, so a server may
/// `give` this handle to a third party who replies straight to the original
/// client (the filesystem forwarding a read to the flash driver, whose bytes
/// never transit the filesystem's address space). Single use is preserved
/// through the transfer, because it is a property of the exchange rather than
/// of whoever holds the handle.
///
/// `NoCopy`, and dropping it without replying is also an answer: the client's
/// `resolve` is told `PeerClosed`. Nothing is stranded.
public struct PipeRequest {
    public(package) handle: PipeRequestHandle,
}

extension PipeRequest: NoCopy {
    /// Drop is release. `reply` and `reply_wait` are `consumes` methods, so a
    /// discharged obligation never reaches this body; what does is one dropped
    /// without a reply, which the client's `resolve` hears as `PeerClosed`.
    func deinit(&var self) {
        if self.handle != NO_HANDLE {
            let _ = sos_handle_release(self.handle)
        }
    }
}

extension PipeRequest {
    /// Send the reply, and discharge the obligation. Requires
    /// `PipeRequestRight.Reply`.
    ///
    /// It consumes this capability whatever it answers, and the type says so.
    /// The declaration carries `consumes` and the call spells the move, `(move
    /// request).reply(body: &msg, len: n)`, so `request` is dead past that
    /// expression and a second reply through the same binding is a compile
    /// error. The kernel's ledger still draws a `BadHandle` fault for a
    /// raw-altitude caller holding a word.
    ///
    /// Nothing is disarmed and nothing is released: the consuming body stands
    /// in for this type's `deinit` body on this path, and the handle word it
    /// leaves behind is swept by a synthesized drop that does nothing.
    ///
    /// `len` may be zero: a reply that carries no data is still an answer, and
    /// it is how the tell idiom's server side discharges (`let _ = (move
    /// request).reply(...)`).
    ///
    /// `Err(SosStatus.PeerClosed)` means the client abandoned its claim and the
    /// reply went nowhere (§2.1's answer, never a silent success). The
    /// obligation is discharged either way, which is what makes ignoring it
    /// with `let _ =` correct rather than careless, and what makes `consumes`,
    /// which consumes on the Err path too, the exact effect for it.
    public func reply(&var self, body: &[UInt8; PIPE_BODY_BYTES],
                      len: UInt) consumes -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
        var none = PipeHandles.none()
        return reply_body(self.handle, &body, len, &var none)
    }

    /// The same reply, carrying handles: §2.1's `send(data?, handles?)` in the
    /// answering direction.
    ///
    /// The carrier is emptied on every path this method can return through: a
    /// reply is discharged whatever it answers and no answer refuses the send
    /// transiently, so there is no retry to keep handles for. `Err(PeerClosed)`
    /// consumes them too: the client abandoned its claim, nothing was staged,
    /// and the kernel releases the carried handles, which may destroy an object
    /// whose last handle this was.
    public func reply_with(&var self, body: &[UInt8; PIPE_BODY_BYTES], len: UInt,
                           handles: &var PipeHandles) consumes -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
        reply_body(self.handle, &body, len, &var handles)
    }

    /// Send the reply and wait for the next thing, in one trap. Requires
    /// `PipeRequestRight.Reply` here and `WaiterRight.Wait` on `waiter`.
    ///
    /// The reply is the primary act and it is what consumes this capability, so
    /// the op belongs to the object being consumed and the wait is the fused
    /// tail. It halves the steady-state server loop (one trap per message
    /// instead of a `reply` and a `wait`) and is the mirror of `PipeInlet.send`
    /// on the client side.
    ///
    /// The waiter is required; there is no degenerate form. The first turn of a
    /// server loop is `waiter.wait()`, reply-without-wait is `reply()`, and the
    /// recovery from a dead client is that same first turn. An optional waiter
    /// would change the return type on a runtime value, which the tree refuses.
    ///
    /// The error names its leg, and `Wait(_)` means the reply landed.
    /// `Err(Reply(s))` is a reply that did not land (the client abandoned its
    /// claim), and the op returned without parking, so one dead client can
    /// never stall the loop; the caller recovers by waiting afresh.
    /// `Err(Wait(s))` is an exchange that completed and a wait that broke
    /// (for example `SosStatus.Revoked`).
    ///
    /// The obligation is discharged on every path (§2.1), which is why this is
    /// a `consumes` method exactly as `reply` is. The call spells the move, the
    /// binding is dead after it, and reaching for it again is a compile error.
    /// Both legs of the error consume: `Err(Reply(_))` did not deliver the
    /// reply and `Err(Wait(_))` did, and neither leaves an obligation anyone
    /// still holds.
    ///
    /// The loop it is written for:
    ///
    /// ```saw
    /// var wake = try waiter.wait() catch { return }
    /// while serving {
    ///     let request = decode(move wake)
    ///     let n = handle(&var out)
    ///     wake = try (move request).reply_wait(body: &out, len: n,
    ///                                          waiter: &waiter)
    ///           catch { ... }
    /// }
    /// ```
    public func reply_wait(&var self, body: &[UInt8; PIPE_BODY_BYTES], len: UInt,
                           waiter: &Waiter
    ) consumes -> Result<WaitResult, ReplyWaitError> {
        var none = PipeHandles.none()
        return reply_wait_body(self.handle, &body, len, waiter.handle, &var none)
    }

    /// The same fused reply, carrying handles. Everything `reply_with` says
    /// about the carrier holds here too: the obligation is discharged on every
    /// path, so the handles go with it, delivered or, on `Err(Reply(_))`,
    /// released.
    public func reply_wait_with(&var self, body: &[UInt8; PIPE_BODY_BYTES],
                                len: UInt, waiter: &Waiter,
                                handles: &var PipeHandles
    ) consumes -> Result<WaitResult, ReplyWaitError> {
        reply_wait_body(self.handle, &body, len, waiter.handle, &var handles)
    }

    /// Mint a sibling of this obligation, narrowed by a keep mask. Everything
    /// `PipeReply.mint` says holds here with `Reply` in `Resolve`'s place,
    /// including the part that matters: `pipe_request_rights()` withholds the
    /// universal `Mint` bit, so this cannot succeed, and calling it terminates
    /// the caller with `AccessDenied`. This is the one-name rule on the
    /// obligation half (`tests/pipe-no-reply`).
    ///
    /// Delegation is untouched, and that is the distinction the rule rests on:
    /// §2.1's forwarding primitive is `Transfer` moving the one name to a third
    /// party (`tests/pipe-delegate`, `tests/pipe-delegate-msg`), never a second
    /// name being made.
    public func mint(&self,
                     rights: UInt32 = ALL_RIGHTS) -> Result<PipeRequest, SosStatus> {
        let raw = try call_value(self.handle, MINT_OP, rights as UInt, 0, 0)
        return PipeRequest(handle: PipeRequestHandle(raw))
    }
}

/// The `unsafe` half of `PipeRequest.reply`, confined to one small named
/// helper. The body's address never leaves it.
///
/// There is no disarm, and nothing takes its place. The caller is a `consumes`
/// method, so its body is the wrapper's teardown on this path: the custom
/// `deinit` never runs, the handle word is swept by a synthesized drop that
/// does nothing, and the binding the caller moved from releases nothing on any
/// path. So this helper needs no wrapper at all; it takes the word, as
/// `post_body`, `call_body` and `take_into_frame` do.
func reply_body(handle: PipeRequestHandle, body: &[UInt8; PIPE_BODY_BYTES],
                len: UInt,
                handles: &var PipeHandles) unsafe -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
    var staged: [UInt64; PIPE_STAGE_DWORDS] = [0; PIPE_STAGE_DWORDS]
    var args = send_record(body_addr_for_kernel(&body, &var staged), len, &handles)
    let raw = sos_pipe_request_reply(handle,
                                     (&var args) as UnsafePointer<UInt>,
                                     pipe_send_record_bytes() as UInt)
    handles.handed_over()
    return checked(raw)
}

/// Which half of a `reply_wait` failed.
///
/// A tier-2 domain enum: cases that carry the leaf status rather than
/// restating its vocabulary, because what a caller needs to know is not a new
/// failure mode but which of two ops produced a familiar one. There is no
/// `TimedOut`, no `Alloc` and no catch-all, because this domain cannot produce
/// them.
///
/// `Wait` means the reply landed, which is the reason the cases are separate. A
/// server loop reads them as two different instructions: `Reply` says this
/// client is gone, recover by waiting afresh; `Wait` says the exchange
/// completed and the wait is what failed. `Wait(NoResource)` and
/// `Wait(QuotaExceeded)` are a delivery whose handles found no room in the
/// table, which a fresh `wait()` after freeing room collects; `Wait(Revoked)`
/// is a Waiter that is gone, which no wait on it can fix.
public enum ReplyWaitError {
    /// The reply did not land: the client abandoned its claim. The obligation
    /// is discharged all the same and nothing parked, so the loop's next act is
    /// its own `waiter.wait()`.
    case Reply(status: SosStatus),
    /// The reply landed and the wait that followed it did not: for example
    /// `SosStatus.Revoked`, the Waiter's last handle gone while this thread
    /// was parked on it.
    case Wait(status: SosStatus),
}

extension ReplyWaitError {
    /// Which leg, in words. The status renders itself.
    public func describe(&self) -> String {
        match self {
            case Reply(_) -> "the reply did not land",
            case Wait(_) -> "the reply landed and the wait failed",
        }
    }
}

extension ReplyWaitError: Printable {
    /// Written out of `append` calls rather than interpolation, which keeps it
    /// alloc-free and therefore usable from a freestanding process. The status
    /// forwards to its own `format`.
    func format(&self, into: &var StringBuilder) {
        try! into.append(self.describe())
        try! into.append(": ")
        match self {
            case Reply(status) -> { status.format(into: &var into) },
            case Wait(status) -> { status.format(into: &var into) },
        }
    }
}

extension ReplyWaitError: Error {}

/// The `unsafe` half of `PipeRequest.reply_wait`, confined to one small named
/// helper. Three addresses cross here and none of them leaves.
///
/// No disarm, for `reply_body`'s reason: the caller is a `consumes` method
/// whose body stands in for the wrapper's teardown, so there is no sentinel to
/// write and no release to suppress. It takes the word.
///
/// The small arguments travel in a record and the body does not. §5.7 gives an
/// op three argument registers and this one has four things to say, so the
/// waiter, the body's address and its length ride the copy-in funnel
/// `Timer.arm` uses, while the wait buffer keeps the register that is left.
/// Putting the body itself in the record would make every call copy
/// `PIPE_BODY_BYTES` in userspace before the trap, a cost the fused path exists
/// to avoid.
func reply_wait_body(handle: PipeRequestHandle, body: &[UInt8; PIPE_BODY_BYTES],
                     len: UInt, waiter: WaiterHandle,
                     handles: &var PipeHandles) unsafe -> Result<WaitResult, ReplyWaitError> {
    var staged: [UInt64; PIPE_STAGE_DWORDS] = [0; PIPE_STAGE_DWORDS]
    var args = PipeReplyWaitRecord(waiter: waiter,
                                   msg: send_record(body_addr_for_kernel(&body, &var staged),
                                                    len, &handles))
    var buf = WaitBuffer(header: [0; WAIT_HEADER_WORDS],
                         meta: PipeMsgMeta(len: 0, kinds: [0; PIPE_MSG_HANDLES]),
                         handles: [NO_HANDLE; WAIT_HANDLE_SLOTS],
                         body: [0; PIPE_BODY_BYTES])
    var leg: UInt = 0
    let raw = sos_pipe_request_reply_wait(handle,
                                          (&var args) as UnsafePointer<UInt>,
                                          pipe_reply_wait_record_bytes() as UInt,
                                          (&var buf.header) as UnsafePointer<UInt>,
                                          (&var leg) as UnsafePointer<UInt>)
    handles.handed_over()
    try checked(raw) catch { return leg_error(error, leg) }
    return decode_wait(&buf)
}

/// Pair a status with the leg the kernel stamped beside it.
///
/// An unknown leg panics, on `decode_wait`'s terms: the kernel wrote it out of
/// the same `ReplyWaitLeg` declaration this function reads, so a value that
/// does not decode is a kernel/`sos` skew rather than data from an untrusted
/// source, and a bug the caller cannot act on should stop the program where it
/// happened.
func leg_error(status: SosStatus, leg: UInt) -> ReplyWaitError {
    guard let which = ReplyWaitLeg.from(raw: leg) else {
        panic("sos: kernel wrote reply-wait leg {}, which this build cannot name",
              leg)
    }
    match which {
        case Reply -> ReplyWaitError.Reply(status: status),
        case Wait -> ReplyWaitError.Wait(status: status),
    }
}

// =============================================================================
// Waitability: the pipe arms, and the consuming attach (spec §2.2's waitable
// list)
// =============================================================================
//
// The overloads live here and not in `sos.waiter`. `Waiter.add` reads its
// argument's handle field, so an overload must see the argument's type; this
// file imports `sos.waiter`, so an overload naming `PipeInlet` written there
// would make `sos.waiter` import its own importer, a cycle. It is the shape
// `Waiter.add(process:)` has in `sos.system`: extension lookup follows the
// facade's `public import`, so the split is invisible at the call site, and
// `waiter.add(outlet:, key:)` reads as one more member of the one list.
//
// The 2x2 is spelled by the verb and the mode: `add` borrows and `give`
// consumes; `AttachMode.Persistent` stays subscribed and `AttachMode.OneShot`
// detaches at delivery. Four combinations, two axes, and no `OneShotConsuming`
// anywhere: the axes are orthogonal, and consuming is an effect change that a
// runtime flag may not make.

extension Waiter {
    /// Watch a connection's server end for traffic: attach it under `key`,
    /// which a `wait()` hands back with an `OutletLevel` when a message is
    /// staged or the client end is gone (spec §2.2's readable arm). Requires
    /// `PipeOutletRight.Wait` there and `WaiterRight.Attach` here.
    ///
    /// This is what turns a poll loop into a park. A server holding several
    /// outlets on one Waiter is §2.2's whole shape: the key says which
    /// connection woke it, and `take` is what it does next.
    ///
    /// `OutletLevel.PeerGone` is drain-then-terminal and arrives only once the
    /// ring is dry: everything the departed client staged is handed over first,
    /// the same order `take`'s own `PeerClosed` follows. Nothing un-closes.
    public func add(&self, outlet: &PipeOutlet, key: UInt,
                    mode: AttachMode = AttachMode.Persistent) -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
        self.attach(outlet.handle, key, mode_flag(mode))
    }

    /// Watch a connection's client end for room: attach it under `key`, which a
    /// `wait()` hands back with an `InletLevel` while the staging ring has a
    /// free slot or the server end is gone. Requires `PipeInletRight.Wait`
    /// there.
    ///
    /// It exists because `post` can never block. A refused poster needs
    /// something better than a poll loop, and this is the missing leg a
    /// caller's blocking `send` composition needs.
    ///
    /// It is a plain level, not a terminal one: consuming it clears nothing,
    /// and the level's end is the poster's own next post. So an attachment left
    /// `Persistent` on a connection with room reports ready every time it is
    /// asked, as an Interrupt's emptiness does.
    public func add(&self, inlet: &PipeInlet, key: UInt,
                    mode: AttachMode = AttachMode.Persistent) -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
        self.attach(inlet.handle, key, mode_flag(mode))
    }

    /// Watch a claim for its answer: attach it under `key`, which a `wait()`
    /// hands back with a `ReplyDelivery` (the reply bytes themselves) when the
    /// server answers, or `PeerClosed` when it never will. Requires
    /// `PipeReplyRight.Wait` there.
    ///
    /// The delivery carries the payload, which makes this attach different in
    /// kind from the others: a client that waits this way never calls
    /// `resolve`, because the answer arrived in the wait record. The
    /// multiplexed client is post -> attach -> wait, three traps, however many
    /// requests are outstanding.
    ///
    /// A delivery is inherently consuming here, whatever `mode` says. A claim
    /// is a one-shot, the answer is spent by the copy-out, and a persistent
    /// subscription to something that has happened for the last time would be a
    /// promise nothing can keep. So the attachment ends at the delivery and
    /// `mode` is inert on this kind. This is stated rather than refused,
    /// because the parameter is uniform across the family and a per-kind
    /// exception would be a rule to remember.
    ///
    /// The handle stays yours with `add`. What a later `resolve` on it answers
    /// is `PeerClosed`: the exchange was discharged by the delivery, and there
    /// is no second copy of an answer. Use `give` when the claim is finished
    /// with, as it is on the success path.
    public func add(&self, reply: &PipeReply, key: UInt,
                    mode: AttachMode = AttachMode.OneShot) -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
        self.attach(reply.handle, key, mode_flag(mode))
    }

    /// Watch an obligation for its cancellation: attach it under `key`, which a
    /// `wait()` hands back with `WaitPayload.Abandoned` once the client has
    /// dropped the claim behind it (spec §2.1's early notice). Requires
    /// `PipeRequestRight.Wait` there.
    ///
    /// Abandonment is information, not an imperative (§2.1). What a server does
    /// with it is the protocol's decision: a cancellable read stops working, a
    /// tell-style message ignores it entirely. Without the attach, a server
    /// learns the same fact when its `reply` answers `PeerClosed`, later and
    /// after the work.
    ///
    /// It is terminal, on the Process arm's terms: a reply column that reached
    /// zero can never rise, so a server attaching after the client gave up is
    /// told at once and nothing un-abandons.
    public func add(&self, request: &PipeRequest, key: UInt,
                    mode: AttachMode = AttachMode.Persistent) -> Result<Void, SosStatus> {
        self.attach(request.handle, key, mode_flag(mode))
    }

    /// Hand a claim to this Waiter, and stop holding it.
    ///
    /// A client that attaches its claim and takes the answer out of the wait
    /// record has no use for the handle afterwards; holding it is a table row
    /// of pure ceremony. `give` says so: the wrapper goes in by move, the
    /// kernel releases the entry as part of the attach, and the attachment owns
    /// the exchange from there. A multiplexer holds zero reply handles however
    /// many requests are outstanding, so its `MAX_HANDLES` stops being the
    /// bound on its in-flight depth.
    ///
    /// Hanging up is destroying the subscription: with no handle left,
    /// `remove(key)` drops the last reference, which is §2.1's cancellation
    /// primitive reached through the Waiter. A handle drop with a live
    /// attachment is not abandonment, because the subscription is the
    /// interested party.
    ///
    /// Everything `Waiter.give(event:)` says about the verb, the gate and the
    /// object-handed-back refusal holds word for word.
    public func give(&self, reply: PipeReply, key: UInt,
                     mode: AttachMode = AttachMode.OneShot
    ) -> Result<Void, (SosStatus, PipeReply)> {
        var owned = move reply
        let word: UInt = owned.handle
        owned.handle = PipeReplyHandle(NO_HANDLE)
        try self.attach_consuming(word, key, mode) catch {
            let back: (SosStatus, PipeReply) = (error, PipeReply(handle: PipeReplyHandle(word)))
            return move back
        }
        return
    }

    /// Hand an obligation to this Waiter: the cancellation watcher that owns
    /// what it watches. Everything `give(reply:)` says holds, with one
    /// consequence worth naming: the obligation is now the attachment's, so
    /// `remove(key)` discharges it and the client's `resolve` answers
    /// `PeerClosed`. A server hangs up on a request by unsubscribing from it.
    public func give(&self, request: PipeRequest, key: UInt,
                     mode: AttachMode = AttachMode.OneShot
    ) -> Result<Void, (SosStatus, PipeRequest)> {
        var owned = move request
        let word: UInt = owned.handle
        owned.handle = PipeRequestHandle(NO_HANDLE)
        try self.attach_consuming(word, key, mode) catch {
            let back: (SosStatus, PipeRequest) = (error, PipeRequest(handle: PipeRequestHandle(word)))
            return move back
        }
        return
    }

    /// Hand a connection's server end to this Waiter. Everything `give(reply:)`
    /// says holds. The subscription owns its end, so `remove(key)` drops the
    /// server end to zero and the connection's clients answer `PeerClosed`.
    ///
    /// The delivery is the take. While a message is staged, a `wait()` that
    /// reports this attachment answers `WaitPayload.Message(msg, request)`:
    /// the kernel takes the message and mints the obligation into the same
    /// record, and the server never calls `take`. Once the ring is dry and
    /// the client end is gone, it answers `Readable(PeerGone)` as an `add`
    /// would.
    public func give(&self, outlet: PipeOutlet, key: UInt,
                     mode: AttachMode = AttachMode.Persistent
    ) -> Result<Void, (SosStatus, PipeOutlet)> {
        var owned = move outlet
        let word: UInt = owned.handle
        owned.handle = PipeOutletHandle(NO_HANDLE)
        try self.attach_consuming(word, key, mode) catch {
            let back: (SosStatus, PipeOutlet) = (error, PipeOutlet(handle: PipeOutletHandle(word)))
            return move back
        }
        return
    }

    /// Hand a connection's client end to this Waiter. The mirror, and the least
    /// useful of these (an end nobody holds cannot be posted through), so it
    /// exists for uniformity and for the one shape that wants it: a watcher
    /// that only cares whether the far side is still there.
    public func give(&self, inlet: PipeInlet, key: UInt,
                     mode: AttachMode = AttachMode.Persistent
    ) -> Result<Void, (SosStatus, PipeInlet)> {
        var owned = move inlet
        let word: UInt = owned.handle
        owned.handle = PipeInletHandle(NO_HANDLE)
        try self.attach_consuming(word, key, mode) catch {
            let back: (SosStatus, PipeInlet) = (error, PipeInlet(handle: PipeInletHandle(word)))
            return move back
        }
        return
    }
}
